  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 1054 PM MDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nAn upper level ridge will dominate our weather through the end of\nthe week with much above normal temperatures. While mostly dry\nweather will prevail, a weak disturbance to our west combined \nwith slight moistening under the ridge will lead to a few late \nday showers. While most of those will be confined to the \nmountains, we can't totally rule out some virga, gusty winds, and\neven a couple light late day showers spreading off the mountains \nonto the Palmer Divide area and adjacent plains. \n\nTuesday will start off with shallow inversions and thus a fairly\nquick warmup is expected again, similar to Monday. Upper level\nclouds will be increasing slightly, but overall do not expect\nenough opacity to limit daytime heating by more than a degree or \ntwo. Thus, forecast highs of 85-90 seem on target across the \nplains with the near 90 degree readings in the South Platte River \nvalley. That puts us near record highs again (For example, \nDenver's record is 87F set in 1975 and 2020 and we should be \nwithin a degree of that, Fort Collins record of 85F set in 1905 \nwill likely be broken).\n\nWhile there is just slight cooling in the column for both\nWednesday and Thursday, temperatures will remain well above\nnormal. We still expect most of the plains and I-25 Corridor to \nreach the lower to mid 80s under the ridge. Once again, can't rule\nout isolated to scattered showers/lone storm mainly over the \nmountains. Friday and Saturday will still be warm and mostly dry \nas we turn to more of a southwest flow aloft in advance of the \nnext storm system. \n\nThat system will dig southward down/near the Pacific Coast Sunday\nand Monday, eventually allowing a cold front to slip southward \nacross the High Plains. It's just that in this pattern with a more\nsoutherly flow aloft it makes timing of these frontal surges \ndifficult to get a handle on. That is clearly pointed out in the \nsudden spread of ensemble temperature forecasts for both Sunday \nand Monday. The same can be said for our precipitation chances  \nwith potential for interaction/incorporation of Hurricane Rachel \nremnants. Overall, the forecast will reflect a cooling trend with\nincreasing chances of showers Sunday through early next week.",
